The Hesperid is the ship of the company that built [[Geography/Hesperid 3|Hesperid 3]] and its fellows Hesperid 1 and Hesperid 2, and the base the company called HQ. It lies on its side on the gray waste at the north end of the rail, a hull miles long with its top shorn away and its floors open to the air.

It is no longer one hull. The ship has been copied six times over around a point in the air and turns through itself in place, pieces swapping and overlapping like a kaleidoscope, so that a single structure shows as six, one for each rail that circles it. A beat sounds as it turns, and something alive is behind the turning.

The ship was cut nearly in half and stranded where it lies, and the company's outposts were set to mine and refine fuel for it. The six-fold working came later, and the same pattern shows on Hesperid 3.

The rail enters what was a cargo hold, now folded. The tarmac road turns a right angle and runs straight up the wall, cones and vehicles stuck flat to it, its painted stripes carrying on across the ceiling; a body that steps onto a wall turns with it, and can walk up and stand on the ceiling. A loading arm reaches into an arriving train for its barrel, and a shack of salvaged board and corrugated metal beside the dock keeps the forms for what comes and goes. A six-wheeled pallet with a barrel rack and no motor or controls sits at the dock.

At the end of the road is an open elevator shaft lined with skulls, turning with the beat. From a great door at its foot pours a sickly iridescent light, the color of the glow over [[Geography/Calaria/index|Calaria]] gone wrong. Between the bones are speaker grates that hiss; a listener who puts an ear to one can pick a voice out of the static warning them to get somewhere else. The light has fallen on the shaft for decades, and its metal struts have come alive as [[Bestiary/Constructs|living incantations]]. The working is a curse past any mortal hand, a god's power turned to a wrong use.

The stations along the rail loop stand at different times: the years 72, 82, 94, 112, 122 and 131. Three barrels of fuel had come in at the station dated 72, one at 94, and five at 131.
